            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the condition  \n\n\\[\n\\text{“for all positive integers }a,b\\text{ with }a+b\\le n+1,\\;(a,b)\\text{ lies on at least one of the lines”}\n\\]\n\nmeans that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) in the right‑angled triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered.  \nWhen \\(n=3\\) this set is\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\},\n\\]\n\ncontaining exactly six points.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\), slope \\(0\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\), slope \\(\\infty\\)), or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\nHence a sunny line must have a slope \\(m\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq 0,\\qquad m\\neq \\infty,\\qquad m\\neq -1 .\n\\]\n\nWe must exhibit three distinct sunny lines (because \\(n=3\\)) whose union contains all points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Selecting admissible pairs of points  \n\nCompute the slopes between all unordered pairs of points of \\(T_3\\).  \nOnly pairs whose slope avoids the three forbidden values can be used, because the line through such a pair would then be sunny.\n\nThe admissible pairs are:\n\n| Pair of points | Slope | Equation of the line |\n|----------------|-------|----------------------|\n| \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(1\\) | \\(y=x\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(-\\dfrac12\\) | \\(y=-\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac52\\) |\n| \\((2,1),(1,3)\\) | \\(-2\\) | \\(y=-2x+5\\) |\n\nAll other pairs give a forbidden slope (either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \nNotice that the three admissible pairs are disjoint; each point of \\(T_3\\) appears in exactly one pair.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Defining the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x, &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2);\\\\[2mm]\nL_2 &: y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52, &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1);\\\\[2mm]\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5, &&\\text{through }(2,1),(1,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes are \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\); none equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nTherefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the three lines are distinct because their slopes differ.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Point‑by‑point verification  \n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line? | Check |\n|-----------------|---------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3\\) ✓ |\n\nEvery point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines, satisfying the covering requirement.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited three distinct sunny lines that together contain all points required for \\(n=3\\).  \nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k=3\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=3\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
