            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nDefine the three “edge’’ subsets of \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{x}&=\\{(1,b):1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nE_{y}&=\\{(a,1):1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nE_{d}&=\\{(a,b):a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese three sets are pairwise intersecting only in the two points\n\\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|E_{x}\\cup E_{y}\\cup E_{d}|=3n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can meet each of the three families in **at most one** point,\nbecause a line parallel to none of the three directions can intersect a\nhorizontal line, a vertical line and a line of slope \\(-1\\) each in at most\none point.  A non‑sunny line, being parallel to one of the families,\ncovers **all** points of that family.\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) given lines only one is non‑sunny.\nThat line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of one of the families, leaving\nat least \\(2n-2\\) points of the other two families uncovered.\nEach of the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most one point of\neach family, i.e. at most \\(2\\) points in total.  Hence the sunny lines\ncan cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points, which is **strictly smaller** than\n\\(2n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  This contradiction shows that at least\ntwo of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny; therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThey cover all points of \\(E_{x}\\cup E_{y}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(4\\le d\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_{d}:x+y=d .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{D_{d}\\}_{d=4}^{n+1}\\) consists of \\(n-2\\) distinct\nnon‑sunny lines; together with (8) they already give a set of \\(n\\) lines\nall of which are non‑sunny (the case \\(k=0\\)).\n\nTo obtain exactly \\(k>0\\) sunny lines we replace \\(k\\) of the diagonals\nby sunny lines.  The only diagonal that needs to be replaced is\n\\(D_{\\,n+2}\\); it is the unique diagonal that contains **more than one**\ninterior point of \\(T_{n}\\).  All other diagonals contain at most one\ninterior point, namely the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and\n\\(a+b=d\\).  Consequently we may keep all diagonals except \\(D_{\\,n+2}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define the interior point  \n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(t+1,\\;n+1-t) .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBoth coordinates of \\(P_{t}\\) are at least \\(2\\) and\n\\(P_{t}\\in T_{n}\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(t+1)+(n+1-t)=n+2\\le n+1\\quad\\text{fails only when }t=0,\n\\]\nso actually \\(P_{t}\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{\\,n+2}\\).\n\nThrough the fixed point \\((1,1)\\) and \\(P_{t}\\) draw the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\;y-1=\\frac{n-t}{t}\\,(x-1) .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{n-t}{t}\\) is a positive rational number different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  Distinct values of\n\\(t\\) give distinct slopes, so the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are\npairwise different.\n\nNow consider the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{D_{d}\\;:\\;4\\le d\\le n+1,\\;d\\neq n+2\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\{S_{t}\\;:\\;1\\le t\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCounting the members of \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n* two lines from (8);\n* the \\(n-2\\) diagonals (9) minus the one we removed, i.e. \\(n-3\\) lines;\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{t}\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|=2+(n-3)+k=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{t}\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the\nrequired value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(a=1\\).  Then \\((a,b)\\in L_{1}\\).\n* **Case 2.** \\(b=1\\).  Then \\((a,b)\\in L_{2}\\).\n* **Case 3.** \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Then \\((a,b)\\in D_{\\,n+1}\\subset\\mathcal L\\).\n* **Case 4.** \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n  Then \\(d:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(4\\le d\\le n\\) and \\(d\\neq n+2\\); consequently\n  \\((a,b)\\in D_{d}\\subset\\mathcal L\\).\n* **Case 5.** \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n  In this situation \\((a,b)=P_{t}\\) for the unique integer\n  \\(t=a-1\\) (which lies between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\) because we have chosen\n  exactly the points \\(P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\)).  By construction\n  \\(P_{t}\\in S_{t}\\subset\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll possibilities are exhausted; therefore every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies\non at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least two\n  non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit family of\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering\n  all points of \\(T_{n}\\) exists (the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) above).\n\nHence the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
