            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n*All three lines non‑sunny* :  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4\n\\]\n\ncover \\(T_{3}\\); hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*Exactly one sunny line* :  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:y=x\n\\]\n\n(the last line has slope \\(1\\)) cover all six points; thus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n*All three lines sunny* :  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:y=x,\\\\\nL_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\nL_{3}&:y=-2x+5,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwith slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny).  \n\\(L_{1}\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,2)\\);  \n\\(L_{2}\\) contains \\((1,2),(3,1)\\);  \n\\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \nHence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n*Two sunny lines* : suppose only one line is non‑sunny.  \nIf that line is vertical \\(x=1\\) (or horizontal \\(y=1\\)), the three points\n\\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) remain. Any line containing two of them would be\nhorizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), none of which is sunny; consequently the\nthird point could not be covered. The same obstruction occurs when the\nnon‑sunny line is a diagonal. Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A necessary upper bound for \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nThe points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) all lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\nIf no vertical (or horizontal) line is used, each of those \\(n\\) points would\nhave to belong to a different sunny line (any two of them would determine a\nvertical line, which is not sunny).  Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total,\nthe remaining points could not be covered.  Hence at least one non‑sunny line\nis required, giving\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ge4). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A configuration with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines (any \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nKeep the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_{s}=(s,\\;n+2-s)\\in T_{n} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nS_{s}:\\;y-1=\\frac{n+1-s}{\\,s-1\\,}(x-1). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m_{s}= \\dfrac{n+1-s}{s-1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus\neach \\(S_{s}\\) is sunny, and the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_{s}\\mid 2\\le s\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which the\n\\(n-1\\) lines \\(S_{s}\\) are sunny; therefore it realises \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(V\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+2\\) then \\((a,b)=P_{a}\\) and belongs to \\(S_{a}\\).\n\n* Otherwise put \\(t=n+1-(a+b)\\) (so \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\)).  \n  A straightforward substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation\n  of \\(S_{a-t}\\); consequently it lies on that sunny line.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(3\\le d\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{d}:\\;x+y=d,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nObserve that for \\(d\\neq n+2\\) the diagonal \\(D_{d}\\) contains at most one of\nthe points \\(P_{s}\\); consequently we may replace any sunny line \\(S_{s}\\) by the\ndiagonal that contains the same point(s) without destroying the covering\nproperty.\n\nLet \\(K\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|K|=k\\). Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(K)=\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_{s}\\mid s\\in K\\}\\cup\n\\{D_{d}\\mid d\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\;d\\neq a+b\\text{ for any }(a,b)=P_{s},\n\\;s\\notin K\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L(K)\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n* the vertical line \\(V\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{s}\\) with \\(s\\in K\\);\n* the remaining \\(n-1-k\\) diagonals, all non‑sunny.\n\nThe same verification as in §3 shows that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at\nleast one line of \\(\\mathcal L(K)\\).  Hence for **every** integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all required points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) (proved in §1).  \n* For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are all integers from\n  \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\) (proved in §§2–4).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
