\\\nL_{3}&: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nChecking each point:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point}&\\text{Line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1}\\;(1=1)\\\\\n(2,2) & L_{1}\\;(2=2)\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{2}\\;(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2}\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{3}\\;(1=-2\\cdot2+5)\\\\\n(1,3) & L_{3}\\;(3=-2\\cdot1+5)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nHence all six points are covered and \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume exactly two of the three lines are sunny.  \nBecause the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) are collinear on the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\), at least one of the three lines must be this vertical line (otherwise we would need three distinct sunny lines to cover those three points).  \nConsequently the remaining two lines are sunny and must cover the four points\n\\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(2,3)\\).  \nAny sunny line that contains two of these points would have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness.  Hence no such configuration exists; \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A universal upper bound\n\nThe points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) all lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf this vertical line were not among the \\(n\\) chosen lines, each of those \\(n\\) points would have to belong to a different sunny line (any two of them determine a vertical line, which is not sunny).  Since only \\(n\\) lines are available, at least one non\u2011sunny line is mandatory.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge4\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny line.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{s}:\\;\\text{through }(s,1)\\text{ and }(1,s+1). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe second point \\((1,s+1)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) because \\(s+1\\le k+2\\le n\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{s}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{s}= \\frac{s+1-1}{1-s}= -\\frac{s}{s-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_{s}\\) is sunny.\nAll the lines \\(V,S_{2},\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\) are distinct, giving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and a total of \\(1+k\\le n\\) lines.  \nTo reach precisely \\(n\\) lines we add any \\(n-1-k\\) further non\u2011sunny lines; for definiteness we may add the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{t}:\\;y=t\\qquad (t=2,3,\\dots ,n-1-k+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich are clearly non\u2011sunny.  The final family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_{s}\\mid 2\\le s\\le k+1\\}\\cup\\{H_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n- k\\},\n\\]\n\nand contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{s}\\) are sunny.\n\n*Verification that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).*\n\n1. **Points with first coordinate \\(1\\).**  \n   Such a point is \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n   It belongs to the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\n2. **Points with second coordinate \\(1\\).**  \n   Such a point is \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   If \\(a=1\\) we are in case\u202f1.  \n   If \\(2\\le a\\le k+1\\) the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) by construction.  \n   If \\(a>k+1\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and the horizontal line \\(H_{a}\\) (which exists because \\(a\\le n-k\\)) contains \\((a,1)\\).\n\n3. **Points with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).**  \n   For such a point we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), hence \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\).  \n   Choose the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\); by definition \\(H_{b}\\) is the line \\(y=b\\) and therefore contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nSince the construction works for any admissible \\(k\\) (with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)), we have proved:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge4\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\text{ there exists a suitable configuration.}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Summary of what remains\n\nThe universal bound (2.1) shows that \\(k\\) can never exceed \\(n-1\\).  \nThe constructions above realise every value \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\) is not settled by the arguments presented here.  This case therefore remains open."

