f \\(n\\) lines; this line contains all remaining points with second coordinate \\(1\\).\n\n*   Let \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).  \n    Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-k\\).  \n    Set \\(i=n+2-b\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\)).  \n    The line \\(S_{i}\\) passes through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i-1,b)\\).  \n    Since the three points \\((a,b),\\;(i-1,b),\\;(i,1)\\) are collinear (they lie on the line of slope \\(-\\dfrac{b}{i-1}\\)), \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_{i}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.  The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, proving that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for the extremal value \\(k=n-1\\) (any \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAgain take the vertical non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) define a sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nS_{a}= \n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{the line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(a-1,n+2-a), & a\\neq n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(2,2), & a=n .\n\\end{cases} \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nIf \\(a\\neq n\\) the slope equals \\(-\\dfrac{n+1-a}{a-1}\\), never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nFor \\(a=n\\) the slope is \\(\\dfrac{1}{2-n}\\), also sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe lines have distinct points on the bottom edge \\((a,1)\\), hence are pairwise different.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n1. **First column.**  \n   Every point \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(V\\).\n\n2. **Points with \\(a\\ge2\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=n+2-a\\)*, then by definition \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_{a}\\) (the case \\(a=n\\) never occurs because then \\(b=2\\) would give \\(a+b=n+2>n+1\\)).\n\n   *If \\(b<n+2-a\\)*, set \\(c=n+2-b\\;(>a)\\).  \n   The line \\(S_{c}\\) (here \\(c\\neq n\\) because \\(b\\ge2\\)) passes through the points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c-1,b)\\).  \n   The three points \\((a,b),\\;(c-1,b),\\;(c,1)\\) are collinear: they lie on the line of slope \\(-\\dfrac{b}{c-1}\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) into the equation of \\(S_{c}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   y-1 = -\\frac{n+1-c}{c-1}\\,(x-c),\n   \\]\n\n   and using \\(c=n+2-b\\) gives an identity, confirming that \\((a,b)\\in S_{c}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_{a}\\mid2\\le a\\le n\\}\\), and exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.  Hence the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBecause a sunny line cannot be vertical, each point \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) must lie on a different line.  \nConsequently there is a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the line \\(L_{a}\\) (with \\(a\\ge2\\)) passes through \\((1,\\sigma(a))\\) and \\((a,1)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L_{a}\\) is \\(\\dfrac{1-\\sigma(a)}{a-1}\\); the line is sunny iff \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\).  Hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement** (no fixed points).\n\nThe line that contains \\((1,1)\\) can intersect each vertical line \\(x=2,\\dots ,n\\) at most once, so it can contain at most \\(n-2\\) interior points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nEvery other line \\(L_{a}\\) can contain at most one interior point (otherwise two interior points would share the same abscissa, forcing the line to be vertical).  \n\nTherefore the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-2)=2n-3 . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(T_{n}\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\ninterior points.  For \\(n\\ge6\\\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
