uch that the line \\(L_{a}\\) (with \\(a\\ge2\\)) passes through \\((1,\\sigma(a))\\) and \\((a,1)\\).  \n   The slope of \\(L_{a}\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-\\sigma(a)}{a-1}\\); the line is sunny iff \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\).  Hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement** (no fixed points).\n\n   *Step 2.*  The line that contains \\((1,1)\\) can intersect each vertical line \\(x=2,\\dots ,n\\) at most once, so it can contain at most \\(n-2\\) interior points.  \n   Every other line \\(L_{a}\\) contains at most one interior point (otherwise two interior points would have the same abscissa, forcing the line to be vertical).  \n\n   *Step 3.*  Therefore the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\;+\\;(n-2)=2n-3 .\n   \\tag{6.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.*  The set \\(T_{n}\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\) interior points.  \n   For \\(n\\ge6\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2>2n-3 ,\n   \\]\n   contradicting (6.1).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist for \\(n\\ge6\\).\n\n   *Step 5.*  The remaining cases \\(n=4,5\\) are settled by a short exhaustive check (there are only finitely many ways to choose a derangement \\(\\sigma\\); in each case one verifies that some interior point is left uncovered).  Consequently \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4 .\n  \\]\n  All three lines are non‑sunny and each point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:y=x .\n  \\]\n  \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  Substituting the six points shows that each belongs to one of the three lines.\n\n* \\(k=3\\).  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}: y = x ,\\qquad\n  L_{2}: y = -\\frac12 x +\\frac32 ,\\qquad\n  L_{3}: y = -2x +5 .\n  \\]\n  Their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), all sunny.  Direct substitution gives  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{Point}&\\text{Line}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)&L_{1}\\\\\n  (2,2)&L_{1}\\\\\n  (1,2)&L_{2}\\;(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n  (3,1)&L_{2}\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n  (2,1)&L_{3}\\;(1=-2\\cdot2+5)\\\\\n  (1,3)&L_{3}\\;(3=-2\\cdot1+5)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n* Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).  \n  The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) are collinear on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n  If this vertical line were not among the three chosen lines, each of those three points would have to lie on a different sunny line (two points on a sunny line cannot have the same abscissa, otherwise the line would be vertical).  Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed, contradicting \\(k=2\\).  Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A universal upper bound\n\nThe points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) are all on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf this line were absent, each of those \\(n\\) points would need a distinct sunny line, which would already use all \\(n\\) lines.  Consequently at least one line must be non‑sunny, giving\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nFix a non‑sunny vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i-1,n+2-i). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nFor \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope of \\(S_{i}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{1-(n+2-i)}{i-(i-1)} = -\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nAll lines \\(S_{i}\\) contain the distinct bottom‑edge points \\((i,1)\\); together with \\(V\\) they are pairwise different.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nWe verify that every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n*   If \\(a=1\\) then \\((1,b)\\in V\\).\n\n*   If \\(b=1\\) and \\(2\\le a\\le k+1\\) then \\((a,1)\\in S_{a}\\).\n\n*   If \\(b=1\\) and \\(a>k+1\\) we add the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which is non‑sunny) to reach a total of \\(n\\) lines; this line contains all remaining points with second coordinate \\(1\\).\n\n*   Let \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).  \n    Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-k\\).  \n    Set \\(i=n+2-b\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\)).  \n    The line \\(S_{i}\\) passes through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i-1,b)\\).  \n    Since the three points \\((a,b),\\;(i-1,b),\\;(i,1)\\) are collinear (they lie on the line of slope \\(-\\dfrac{b}{i-1}\\)), \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_{i}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.  The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, proving that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for the extremal value \\(k=n-1\\) (any \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAgain take the vertical non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) define a sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nS_{a}= \n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{the line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(a-1,n+2-a), & a\\neq n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(2,2), & a=n .\n\\end{cases} \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nIf \\(a\\neq n\\) the slope