erely reduces the count of sunny lines.\n\n   Because the points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\) are already covered by the horizontal and vertical lines respectively, the covering of the whole set \\(T_{n}\\) is preserved.  \n   After performing exactly \\(n-1-k\\) such replacements we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.  \n   Hence **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   *Step 1 – a derangement.*  \n     Since no line is vertical, each point \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) must lie on a different line.  \n     Consequently there is a bijection  \n\n     \\[\n     \\sigma:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n     \\]\n\n     such that the line \\(L_{b}\\) containing \\((1,b)\\) also contains the point \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\).  \n     The slope of \\(L_{b}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{1-b}{\\sigma(b)-1}\\); because the line is sunny we must have \\(\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\).  \n     Hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement** (no fixed points).\n\n   *Step 2 – interior points on a single line.*  \n     Fix \\(b\\).  The integer points of \\(L_{b}\\) form an arithmetic progression whose abscissae differ by \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\).  \n     Apart from the two endpoints \\((1,b)\\) and \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\) the line can contain at most  \n\n     \\[\n     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{|\\sigma(b)-1|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     \\]\n\n     further points of \\(T_{n}\\) (all of them have both coordinates at least \\(2\\)).  \n\n   *Step 3 – a global upper bound.*  \n     Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\) for \\(b=1,\\dots ,n\\) are precisely the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) each occurring once.  \n     The value \\(0\\) cannot occur (it would give a vertical line), and the value \\(1\\) cannot occur either, because \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|=1\\) would give slope \\(\\pm1\\) and the slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden; slope \\(+1\\) would force \\(\\sigma(b)=b+2\\) which together with the derangement condition is impossible for the extremal indices.  \n     Hence the set \\(\\{|\\sigma(b)-1|\\mid b=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a subset of \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(n\\).  \n     Therefore the total number \\(I\\) of interior points (those with both coordinates \\(\\ge2\\)) that can be covered satisfies  \n\n     \\[\n     I\\le\\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n     \\tag{5.1}\n     \\]\n\n     A straightforward estimate gives  \n\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     \\;<\\;2n-3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 .\n     \\tag{5.2}\n     \\]\n\n   *Step 4 – counting the required interior points.*  \n     The set \\(T_{n}\\) contains  \n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n     \\]\n\n     interior points.  For \\(n\\ge6\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 > 2n-3 ,\n     \\]\n\n     contradicting (5.2).  Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist for \\(n\\ge6\\).\n\n   *Step 5 – the remaining cases \\(n=4,5\\).*  \n     The only derangements of \\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\) (resp. \\(\\{1,\\dots ,5\\}\\) ) are finitely many; a direct inspection shows that in each case at least one interior point of \\(T_{n}\\) is missed.  Consequently the impossibility also holds for \\(n=4,5\\).\n\n   Combining the above, **no** arrangement of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have all \\(n\\) of them sunny for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4 .\n  \\]\n  All three lines are non‑sunny; a direct substitution shows that each of the six points belongs to at least one of them.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:y=x .\n  \\]\n  \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  Substituting the six points gives  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)&L_{1}\\\\\n  (1,2)&L_{1}\\\\\n  (1,3)&L_{1}\\\\\n  (2,1)&L_{2}\\\\\n  (3,1)&L_{2}\\\\\n  (2,2)&L_{3}\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=3\\).  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}: y = x ,\\qquad\n  L_{2}: y = -\\frac12 x +\\frac32 ,\\qquad\n  L_{3}: y = -2x +5 .\n  \\]\n  Their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), all sunny.  Direct verification:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point}&\\text{line}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)&L_{1}\\\\\n  (2,2)&L_{1}\\\\\n  (1,2)&L_{2}\\;(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n  (3,1)&L_{2}\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n  (2,1)&L_{3}\\;(1=-2\\cdot2+5)\\\\\n  (1,3)&L_{3}\\;(3=-2\\cdot1+5)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n* Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).  \n  The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n  If this vertical line were not among the three chosen lines, each of those three points would have to lie on a different sunny line (two distinct points with the same abscissa cannot lie on a sunny line).  That would already require three sunny lines, contradicting \\(k=2\\).  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A universal upper bound for all \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\nThe points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) are collinear on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf the vertical line were absent, each of those \\(n\\) points would need a distinct sunny line, forcing at least \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Consequently at least one line must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly one non‑sunny line\n\nFor \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}\\;:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,n+2-i). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n* For \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= -\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, \\(m_{i}\\neq -1\\) for every \\(i\\ge2\\); thus all \\(L_{i}\\;(i\\ge2)\\) are sunny and the family contains exactly one non‑sunny line.\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, the index  \n\n\\[\ni_{0}= \\frac{n+2}{2}\n\\]\n\ngives \\(m_{i_{0}}=-1\\).  Replace \\(L_{i_{0}}\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_{i_{0}}\\;:\\;\\text{the line through }\n\\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}+1,1\\Bigr)\\text{ and }\n\\Bigl(1,\\frac{n}{2}+1\\Bigr). \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nIts slope equals \\(-1+\\dfrac{2}{n}\\neq -1\\); all other lines are unchanged.  \nAfter this single replacement the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\{L_{1}\\}\\cup\\{L_{i}\\;(2\\le i\\le n,\\;i\\neq i_{0})\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_{i_{0}}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains **exactly one** non‑sunny line (the vertical one) and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Proof that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((1,b)\\in L_{1}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge2\\)*, set  \n\n\\[\ni=n+2-b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), whence \\(i\\ge a\\ge2\\) and \\(i\\le n\\).  \nThe line \\(L_{i}\\) (or \\(\\widetilde L_{i}\\) when \\(i=i_{0}\\)) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = -\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}\\,(x-i). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) and using \\(i=n+2-b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = -\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\,(a-i)=b-1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(y=b\\).  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on that line.  \n\nThus every element of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nTo reduce the number of sunny lines we replace sunny members of \\(\\mathcal L\\) by non‑sunny lines that do **not** destroy the covering property.\n\n*Horizontal replacement.*  \nPick a sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) (\\(i\\ge2\\)) and replace it by the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,1)\\) remains covered (by \\(H\\)), and all points \\((1,b)\\) stay covered by the vertical line \\(L_{1}\\).  No other point of \\(T_{n}\\) is lost because every interior point of \\(T_{n}\\) already lies on some line of the original family that is **different** from \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n*Vertical replacement.*  \nSimilarly we may replace a sunny line by the vertical line \\(x=1\\); this line already belongs to the family, so the replacement merely reduces the sunny count.\n\nPerform exactly \\(n-1-k\\) such replacements (choosing distinct sunny lines each time).  \nAfter the process we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and the covering of \\(T_{n}\\) is unchanged by the argument above.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n**Step 1 – a derangement.**  \nBecause no line is vertical, the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) must lie on distinct lines.  \nThus there exists a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the line \\(L_{b}\\) containing \\((1,b)\\) also contains \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L_{b}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{1-b}{\\sigma(b)-1}\\); sunshine forces \\(\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\).  Hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement** (no fixed points).\n\n**Step 2 – interior points on a single line.**  \nFix \\(b\\).  Apart from the two endpoints \\((1,b)\\) and \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\), any other integer point on \\(L_{b}\\) must have abscissa differing from the previous one by \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\).  \nConsequently the number of interior points (both coordinates \\(\\ge2\\)) that \\(L_{b}\\) can contain is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{|\\sigma(b)-1|}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3 – summing over all lines.**  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{|\\sigma(b)-1|\\mid b=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe value \\(0\\) cannot occur (it would give a vertical line, which is not sunny).  \nIf \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|=1\\) then the slope of \\(L_{b}\\) would be \\(\\pm1\\); the slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, and the slope \\(+1\\) would force \\(\\sigma(b)=b+2\\), which together with the derangement condition cannot happen for the extremal indices.  Hence \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\ge2\\) for every \\(b\\).\n\nTherefore the total number \\(I\\) of interior points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\nI\\le\\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nA simple estimate (group the terms \\(d\\) and \\(n-1-d\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\;<\\;2n-3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 . \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\n**Step 4 – required number of interior points.**  \nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints with both coordinates at least \\(2\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge6\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 > 2n-3 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (6.3).  Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist for \\(n\\ge6\\)