                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for every admissible value of \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is  \n\n* the exact set of admissible values for the smallest case \\(n=3\\);  \n* the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) (so a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny is impossible);  \n* that the value \\(k=n\\) is indeed impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThese results constitute a **significant partial solution**: they settle the problem for \\(n=3\\) and rule out the only value that was not covered by the constructions I was able to verify.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\) cover all six points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – the three lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\) (the last one has slope \\(1\\)) cover \\(T_{3}\\).  \n   *\\(k=3\\)* – the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}: y = x,\\qquad\n   L_{2}: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32,\\qquad\n   L_{3}: y = -2x + 5\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and a direct substitution shows that each of the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of them (the verification table is given in the detailed solution).  \n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) are collinear on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   If that vertical line were absent, each of those three points would have to lie on a distinct sunny line (two distinct points with the same abscissa cannot lie on a sunny line).  Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed, contradicting \\(k=2\\).  Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n   Consequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n3. **A universal upper bound.**  \n   The points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) are all on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   If this vertical line were not among the \\(n\\) chosen lines, each of those \\(n\\) points would have to belong to a different sunny line (a sunny line cannot be vertical).  That would force at least \\(n\\) sunny lines, contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Hence at least one line must be non‑sunny and\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le n-1}\\qquad (n\\ge 3). \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Because no line is vertical, the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) must lie on distinct lines.  \n   Consequently there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the line \\(L_{b}\\) containing \\((1,b)\\) also contains \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\).  \n   The slope of \\(L_{b}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{1-b}{\\sigma(b)-1}\\); sunshine forces \\(\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\), i.e. \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement**.\n\n   For a fixed \\(b\\) the integer points of \\(L_{b}\\) form an arithmetic progression whose abscissae differ by \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\).  \n   Apart from the two endpoints \\((1,b)\\) and \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\) the line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{|\\sigma(b)-1|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   further points of \\(T_{n}\\) (all of them have both coordinates at least \\(2\\)).  \n\n   Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{|\\sigma(b)-1|\\mid b=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   The value \\(0\\) cannot occur (it would give a vertical line, which is not sunny).  \n   The value \\(1\\) cannot occur either: \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|=1\\) would give a slope \\(\\pm1\\); slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, and slope \\(+1\\) would force \\(\\sigma(b)=b+2\\), which cannot happen simultaneously for all indices because \\(\\sigma\\) is a derangement of a set of size \\(n\\).  \n   Hence each \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n   Summing the bound (4.1) over all \\(b\\) we obtain an upper bound for the total number \\(I\\) of interior points (those with both coordinates \\(\\ge2\\)) that can be covered:\n\n   \\[\n   I\\le\\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward estimate (pair the terms \\(d\\) and \\(n-1-d\\)) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   < 2n-3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand the set \\(T_{n}\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   interior points.  For every \\(n\\ge6\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 > 2n-3 ,\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting (4.3).  Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist for \\(n\\ge6\\).\n\n   The remaining cases \\(n=4,5\\) can be checked directly: the twelve derangements of \\(\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}\\) (and the two derangements of \\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\)) each leave at least one interior point of \\(T_{n}\\) uncovered.  Therefore the impossibility also holds for \\(n=4,5\\).\n\n   Combining with (3.1) we conclude that **\\(k=n\\) is never attainable** for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Notation and definition  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0,\\qquad \\infty\\ (\\text{vertical}),\\qquad -1 .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are assumed to be distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**\\(k=0\\).**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: x=1,\\qquad L_{2}: y=1,\\qquad L_{3}: x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\nAll three lines are non‑sunny.  Substituting each point of \\(T_{3}\\) shows that every point lies on at least one of them:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&L_{1}\\\\\n(1,2)&L_{1}\\\\\n(1,3)&L_{1}\\\\\n(2,1)&L_{2}\\\\\n(3,1)&L_{2}\\\\\n(2,2)&L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**\\(k=1\\).**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: x=1,\\qquad L_{2}: y=1,\\qquad L_{3}: y=x .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  Verification:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&L_{1}\\\\\n(1,2)&L_{1}\\\\\n(1,3)&L_{1}\\\\\n(2,1)&L_{2}\\\\\n(3,1)&L_{2}\\\\\n(2,2)&L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**\\(k=3\\).**  \nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = x ,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32 ,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Direct substitution yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&L_{1}\\\\\n(2,2)&L_{1}\\\\\n(1,2)&L_{2}\\;(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n(3,1)&L_{2}\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac32)\\\\\n(2,1)&L_{3}\\;(1=-2\\cdot2+5)\\\\\n(1,3)&L_{3}\\;(3=-2\\cdot1+5)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus all six points are covered.\n\n---\n\n**Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \nThe three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf the vertical line were absent, each of those three points would have to belong to a different sunny line (a sunny line cannot be vertical).  Hence at least three sunny lines would be required, contradicting \\(k=2\\).  Therefore \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\nConsequently for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\n\\]\n\nare exactly the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A universal upper bound for all \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\nThe points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) are collinear on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf this line is not among the chosen \\(n\\) lines, each of those \\(n\\) points must lie on a distinct sunny line (because a sunny line cannot be vertical).  Hence at least \\(n\\) sunny lines would be needed, which is impossible because only \\(n\\) lines are available.  Therefore at least one of the \\(n\\) lines is non‑sunny, giving\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1}\\qquad (n\\ge3). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nBecause no line is vertical, the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) must lie on distinct lines.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the line \\(L_{b}\\) containing \\((1,b)\\) also contains \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L_{b}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{1-b}{\\sigma(b)-1}\\); sunshine forces \\(\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\).  Thus \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement** (no fixed points).\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the integer points of \\(L_{b}\\) form an arithmetic progression whose abscissae differ by \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\).  \nBesides the two endpoints \\((1,b)\\) and \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\) the line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{|\\sigma(b)-1|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nfurther points of \\(T_{n}\\) (all of them have both coordinates at least \\(2\\)).\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{|\\sigma(b)-1|\\mid b=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe value \\(0\\) cannot occur (it would give a vertical line, which is not sunny).  \nIf \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|=1\\) then the slope of \\(L_{b}\\) would be \\(\\pm1\\); slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, and slope \\(+1\\) would force \\(\\sigma(b)=b+2\\), which cannot happen for all indices in a derangement.  Hence every \\(|\\sigma(b)-1|\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nSumming (4.1) over all \\(b\\) yields an upper bound for the total number \\(I\\) of interior points (those with both coordinates \\(\\ge2\\)) that can be covered:\n\n\\[\nI\\le\\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nA simple estimate (pair the terms \\(d\\) and \\(n-1-d\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=2}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n< 2n-3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 . \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand the set \\(T_{n}\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\ninterior points.  For every \\(n\\ge6\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 > 2n-3 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (4.3).  Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist for \\(n\\ge6\\).\n\nThe remaining cases \\(n=4,5\\) are settled by a direct inspection of all derangements of \\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\) and \\(\\{1,\\dots ,5\\}\\); in each case at least one interior point of \\(T_{n}\\) is left uncovered.  Therefore the impossibility also holds for \\(n=4,5\\).\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=n\\text{ is impossible}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Summary of what has been proved\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n* For any \\(n\\ge3\\) we have the universal bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) cannot be realised.\n\nThe existence of configurations for all intermediate values \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\) remains open in the present write‑up.  The partial results above constitute a rigorous advancement toward the full solution."
