            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0,\\qquad \\infty\\ (\\text{vertical}),\\qquad -1 .\n\\]\n\nAll lines in the construction are required to be distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A universal non‑sunny line  \n\nThe points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\n\\]\n\nare collinear on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf this line were absent, each of those \\(n\\) points would have to belong to a different sunny line (a sunny line cannot be vertical).  \nThus at least one of the \\(n\\) chosen lines must be non‑sunny, and we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(1,2),\\qquad P_{3}=(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line containing two of them is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are **non‑sunny**.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.\n\nIf exactly one line among the \\(n\\) is allowed to be non‑sunny (the situation \\(k=n-1\\)), at most two of the three points can be covered, leaving the third uncovered – a contradiction.  \nHence for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nAs observed above, a sunny line can contain at most one of the set \\(\\{P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\}\\); therefore at least three distinct sunny lines are needed to cover these three points, leaving at most \\(n-3\\) sunny lines for the remaining points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThe number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose both coordinates are at least 2 (the “interior” points) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 > n-3 .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n-3\\) remaining sunny lines cannot possibly cover all interior points, a contradiction.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk=n\\ \\text{is never attainable.}\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for all admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.1  The columns that will stay vertical  \n\nKeep the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and together they cover all points whose abscissa \\(a\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.2  The right‑most \\(k\\) columns  \n\nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_{j}= \\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a=n+1-k+j\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe height of column \\(C_{j}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nh_{j}=n+1-(n+1-k+j)=k+1-j\\le k .\n\\]\n\nHence each column \\(C_{j}\\) contains at most \\(k\\) points.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.3  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1). \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) is \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) the line is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.4  Points of the right‑most columns that lie on the sunny lines  \n\nTake a column \\(C_{j}\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le k\\)) and a line \\(L_{i}\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \nSubstituting the abscissa \\(a=n+1-k+j\\) into (5.3) gives the ordinate  \n\n\\[\nb_{i,j}= i\\bigl(n+1-k+j\\bigr)-(i-1)= i\\,(n-k+j)+1 . \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le i,j\\le k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b_{i,j}\\le i\\,(n-k+k)+1 = i\\,n+1\\le n+1-(n+1-k+j),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Therefore the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{i,j}= \\bigl(n+1-k+j,\\; i\\,(n-k+j)+1\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(T_{n}\\) and lies on \\(L_{i}\\).\n\nFor a fixed column \\(C_{j}\\) the \\(k\\) numbers \\(b_{i,j}\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\) are distinct (they differ by multiples of \\(n-k+j\\)), so the points \\(\\{P_{i,j}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) are distinct.  \nSince the column \\(C_{j}\\) contains exactly \\(h_{j}=k+1-j\\) points and the set \\(\\{P_{i,j}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) contains at least \\(h_{j}\\) points (the first \\(h_{j}\\) values of \\(i\\) already give distinct points inside the column), we conclude that **every** point of \\(C_{j}\\) is among the points \\(P_{i,j}\\).  Consequently each column \\(C_{j}\\) is completely covered by the family \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.5  Counting the lines  \n\nThe family consists of  \n\n* the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5.2) – all non‑sunny, and  \n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines (5.3).\n\nAll lines are distinct, their total number is \\(n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nBy the discussion above every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.6  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n* \\(k=1\\): the lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\) (the last one is sunny).  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n  \\]\n\n  which were verified point‑by‑point in the detailed part.  \n\nThe case \\(k=2\\) is impossible: the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) force at least three non‑sunny lines, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility results (Sections 2–4) with the explicit constructions (Section 5) we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n\\ge4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nAll these values are attainable, and no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
