ine is omitted, each of these points must lie on some line that is **not vertical**. The solution asserts that such a line must be sunny, but a non\u2011sunny line could be horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)). Both types can contain at most one of the points \\((1,b)\\) because the \\(y\\)-coordinate would be forced constant. Hence each point would need a distinct line, regardless of whether the line is sunny or non\u2011sunny. The conclusion \\(k\\le n-1\\) is therefore true, but the reasoning given does not address the horizontal and slope\u202f\\(-1\\) possibilities.  \n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the step\u2019s conclusion is correct, but the justification is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\) (Section\u202f3)\n\n> \u201cConsider the three points \\(P_1=(1,1), P_2=(1,2), P_3=(2,1)\\). Any line containing two of them is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non\u2011sunny. Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points. If exactly one line among the \\(n\\) is allowed to be non\u2011sunny (the situation \\(k=n-1\\)), at most two of the three points can be covered, leaving the third uncovered \u2013 a contradiction. Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) \\(k\\le n-2\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe three points are correctly identified. The pairwise lines are indeed vertical, horizontal, or slope\u202f\\(-1\\). Therefore a sunny line can contain at most one of them. With only one non\u2011sunny line available, at most two of the three points can be covered (the non\u2011sunny line can cover at most two of them, and the remaining sunny line can cover at most one). Since we need all three points covered, a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line is impossible. The argument is sound for any \\(n\\ge3\\); the restriction to \\(n\\ge4\\) is unnecessary but does not affect correctness.\n\n**Classification:** **Correct