ints whose abscissa \\(a\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThese vertical lines indeed cover all points in the first \\(n-k\\) columns. No issue here.\n\n**Classification:** **Correct**.\n\n#### 5.2  The right\u2011most \\(k\\) columns (Section\u202f5.2)\n\n> \u201cFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let \\(C_{j}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a=n+1-k+j\\}\\). The height of column \\(C_{j}\\) equals \\(h_{j}=k+1-j\\le k\\). Hence each column \\(C_{j}\\) contains at most \\(k\\) points.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nFor a fixed column \\(a=n+1-k+j\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(b\\le k-j\\). Thus the number of admissible \\(b\\) values is \\(k-j\\) (if \\(k-j\\ge1\\)), not \\(k+1-j\\). The formula for \\(h_j\\) is off by one, and for \\(j=k\\) the column is actually empty (since \\(b\\le0\\)). Consequently the claim that each column contains at most \\(k\\) points is true but the stated bound \\(h_j=k+1-j\\) is incorrect. This mis\u2011calculation undermines the subsequent counting argument.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the column height is mis\u2011computed, which invalidates the later steps that rely on this value.\n\n#### 5.3  Sunny lines (Section\u202f5.3)\n\n> \u201cFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line \\(L_{i}:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1).\\) The slope of \\(L_{i}\\) is \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) the line is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe lines are indeed sunny and distinct. However, the solution provides **no justification** that these particular lines intersect the points of the right\u2011most columns. The choice appears arbitrary; the subsequent verification (Section\u202f5.4) attempts to justify it but contains errors.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the step lacks a rigorous argument that the lines will cover the required points.\n\n#### 5.4  Points of the right\u2011most columns that lie on the sunny lines (Section\u202f5.4)\n\n> \u201cSubstituting the abscissa \\(a=n+1-k+j\\) into (5.3) gives the ordinate  \n> \\(b_{i,j}= i\\bigl(n+1-k+j\\bigr)-(i-1)= i\\,(n-k+j)+1.\\)  \n> Because \\(1\\le i,j\\le k\\) we have \\(1\\le b_{i,j}\\le i\\,(n-k+k)+1 = i\\,n+1\\le n+1-(n+1-k+j)\\), which is precisely the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Therefore the point \\(P_{i,j}= \\bigl(n+1-k+j,\\; i\\,(n-k+j)+1\\bigr)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) and lies on \\(L_{i}\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe algebraic manipulation is incorrect. Starting from \\(b_{i,j}= i\\,(n-k+j)+1\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\\[\n(n+1-k+j) + i\\,(n-k+j)+1 \\le n+1,\n\\]  \nwhich simplifies to  \n\\[\ni\\,(n-k+j) + (n+2-k+j) \\le n+1,\n\\]  \nor  \n\\[\ni\\,(n-k+j) \\le -1 - (2-k+j),\n\\]  \nwhich is impossible for positive \\(i\\) and \\(j\\). In fact, for many choices (e.g. \\(n=5,\\;k=2,\\;i=1,\\;j=1\\)) we obtain \\(b_{1,1}=1\\cdot(5-2+1)+1=5\\), giving the point \\((5,5)\\) which does **not** satisfy \\(5+5\\le6\\). Hence the claimed inequality \u201c\\(b_{i,j}\\le n+1-(n+1-k+j)\\)\u201d is false; the right\u2011hand side is \\(k-j\\), while the left\u2011hand side is typically much larger. Consequently the points \\(P_{i,j}\\) are **outside** the triangle \\(T_n\\) for most indices, and the construction fails to cover the intended columns.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the verification is mathematically incorrect; the construction does not achieve the required coverage.\n\n#### 5.5  Counting the lines (Section\u202f5.5)\n\n> \u201cThe family consists of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (non\u2011sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (5.3). All lines are distinct, their total number is \\(n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. By the discussion above every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBecause the discussion in Section\u202f5.4 is false, the claim that \u201cevery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines\u201d is unsupported. The counting of lines themselves is correct, but the coverage claim is invalid.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the conclusion relies on the flawed argument of Section\u202f5.4.\n\n#### 5.6  The special case \\(n=3\\) (Section\u202f5.6)\n\n> \u201c\\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n> \\]  \n> which were verified point\u2011by\u2011point in the detailed part.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe second line \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\) does **not** pass through any point of \\(T_3\\) other than \\((1,1)\\). Substituting the six points of \\(T_3\\) shows that \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are not covered. The correct line that would cover those points is \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\). Hence the provided construction is erroneous, and the claim of a point\u2011by\u2011point verification is false. Although a correct configuration of three sunny lines exists (e.g. \\(y=x,\\; y=2x-1,\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\)), the solution does not present it.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the construction is incorrect and the claimed verification is absent; the conclusion that \\(k=3\\) is attainable is not justified by the given argument.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Overall Consistency\n\nThe solution simultaneously asserts in Section\u202f4 that \u201c\\(k=n\\) is never attainable\u201d and in Section\u202f5.6 that \u201c\\(k=3\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\) for \\(n=3\\)) is attainable.\u201d This direct contradiction highlights a **critical logical inconsistency**.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 contradictory statements invalidate the overall argument.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe solution fails to provide a correct proof of the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Section\u202f4) and presents a flawed construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) (Section\u202f5). Moreover, the special\u2011case construction for \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\) is erroneous, and the overall answer contradicts itself. Consequently the answe