            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines we shall use are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A necessary non‑sunny line  \n\nThe points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\n\\]\n\nlie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf this line were omitted, each of those \\(n\\) points would have to belong to a different sunny line, because a sunny line cannot be vertical.  \nHence at least one of the \\(n\\) chosen lines is non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  At least two non‑sunny lines  \n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(1,2),\\qquad P_{3}=(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line containing two of the three points is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \n\nIf only one line were allowed to be non‑sunny (the case \\(k=n-1\\)), at most two of the three points could be covered, contradicting the covering requirement.  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(k=n-2\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\) is impossible  \n\nAssume \\(n\\ge5\\) and \\(k=n-2\\); then exactly two lines are non‑sunny.  \nLook at the two right‑most columns of the triangle:\n\n* column \\(x=n-1\\) contains the points \\((n-1,1),\\dots ,(n-1,n-2)\\) – altogether \\(n-2\\) points;\n* column \\(x=n\\) contains the single point \\((n,1)\\).\n\nA sunny line can meet a given column in **at most one** point; therefore the \\(n-2\\) points of the column \\(x=n-1\\) require at least \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nBecause we have exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, each of them must contain one of those points.\n\nNow consider the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \nIf it were placed on a sunny line already used for a point of the column \\(x=n-1\\), that line would contain the two points \\((n-1,t)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) for some \\(t\\).  \nThe slope of such a line equals \\(\\dfrac{1-t}{1}=1-t\\).  \nSince \\(t\\ge1\\), the slope is either \\(0\\) (when \\(t=1\\)) or a negative integer \\(\\le-1\\).  \nThe only admissible negative slope is \\(-1\\), which is forbidden; the slope \\(0\\) corresponds to a horizontal line, also forbidden.  \nHence \\((n,1)\\) cannot lie on any of the already used sunny lines and would need a third sunny line – a contradiction.\n\nTherefore for every \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Existence for the remaining values  \n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.1  \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)  \n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n}.\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.2  \\(k=1\\)  \n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\ntogether with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = x-(n-1)}\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(a=n-1\\) we have the point \\((n-1,1)\\) on the sunny line, and for \\(a=n\\) the point \\((n,2)\\) (which also belongs to \\(T_{n}\\)).  \nAll other points of \\(T_{n}\\) have abscissa at most \\(n-1\\) and are therefore covered by the vertical lines.  \nThus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.3  \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=4\\)  \n\nThe four lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x-y=2,\\;x-y=3}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy the requirements.  \nBoth lines \\(x-y=2\\) and \\(x-y=3\\) have slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  \nA direct check shows\n\n* \\(x-y=2\\) contains \\((3,1)\\) and \\((4,2)\\);\n* \\(x-y=3\\) contains \\((4,1)\\);\n* the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) cover all remaining points of \\(T_{4}\\).\n\nHence exactly two sunny lines occur.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.4  \\(k=3\\) when \\(n=3\\)  \n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), all admissible.  \nSubstituting the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) shows that each point satisfies at least one of the three equations; consequently the configuration works and uses three sunny lines.\n\n---\n\nAll the constructions above are straightforward verifications; each line is distinct and its slope avoids the forbidden values \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Completion of the classification  \n\nCollecting the impossibility results (Sections 2–4) and the explicit constructions (Section 5) we obtain the full list of admissible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n=4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n\\ge5:\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nNo other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur, and for every listed pair \\((n,k)\\) a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions exists.  This completes the determination.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
