vering requirement.  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(k=n-2\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\) is impossible  \n\nAssume \\(n\\ge5\\) and \\(k=n-2\\); then exactly two lines are non‑sunny.  \nLook at the two right‑most columns of the triangle:\n\n* column \\(x=n-1\\) contains the points \\((n-1,1),\\dots ,(n-1,n-2)\\) – altogether \\(n-2\\) points;\n* column \\(x=n\\) contains the single point \\((n,1)\\).\n\nA sunny line can meet a given column in **at most one** point; therefore the \\(n-2\\) points of the column \\(x=n-1\\) require at least \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nBecause we have exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, each of them must contain one of those points.\n\nNow consider the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \nIf it were placed on a sunny line already used for a point of the column \\(x=n-1\\), that line would contain the two points \\((n-1,t)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) for some \\(t\\).  \nThe slope of such a line equals \\(\\dfrac{1-t}{1}=1-t\\).  \nSince \\(t\\ge1\\), the slope is either \\(0\\) (when \\(t=1\\)) or a negative integer \\(\\le-1\\).  \nThe only admissible negative slope is \\(-1\\), which is forbidden; the slope \\(0\\) corresponds to a horizontal line, also forbidden.  \nHence \\((n,1)\\) cannot lie on any of the already used sunny lines and would need a third sunny line – a contradiction.\n\nTherefore for every \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Existence for the remaining values  \n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.1  \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)  \n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n}.\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.2  \\(k=1\\)  \n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) 