+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-1\\) and contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(D\\) were omitted, each of those \\(n\\) points would have to lie on a different line, which would already use all \\(n\\) available lines and leave no line for the remaining points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nHence **\\(D\\) must belong to any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines**.\n\n*First column.*  \nThe first column  \n\n\\[\nC_{1}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points, and only \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(D\\).  \nConsequently at least one further line is needed to cover the other \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_{1}\\).  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(C_{1}\\) in at most one point, whereas a vertical line meets it in all its points.  \nThus the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1\n\\]\n\nmust also be present.\n\nTherefore every admissible configuration contains the two non‑sunny lines \\(D\\) and \\(V\\).  \nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No configuration with two or more sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge4\\) and that at least two of the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are sunny.  \nDenote by \\(\\mathcal L\\) the set of those \\(n-2\\) lines (both sunny and possibly non‑sunny).\n\n*Column 2.*  \nColumn 2 consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nC_{2}=\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly one of them, \\((2,n-1)\\), lies on the diagonal \\(D\\); the other \\(n-2\\) points are not on any forced line.  \n\nA line that is **not** vertical meets column 2 in at most one point; a vertical line would be \\(V\\), which does not intersect column 2.  \nHence **each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain at most one point of \\(C_{2}\\setminus\\{(2,n-1)\\}\\).**  \n\nBecause there are precisely \\(n-2\\) such points, every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain *exactly one* of them.\n\nConsequently the two sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) each contain a distinct point of column 2.\n\n*Column 3.*  \nColumn 3 is  \n\n\\[\nC_{3}=\\{(3,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-2\\},\n\\]\n\nand \\((3,n-2)\\) lies on \\(D\\).  \nAgain each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can meet column 3 in at most one point.  \n\nSuppose a sunny line \\(S\\in\\mathcal L\\) also contained a point of \\(C_{3}\\).  \nThen \\(S\\) would meet column 2 in two distinct points (the one from \\(C_{2}\\) guaranteed by the previous paragraph and the new one from \\(C_{3}\\)), which is impossible because a non‑vertical line can intersect a given column only once.  \nHence **both sunny lines avoid column 3 altogether**.\n\nBut column 3 still contains \\(n-3\\ge1\\) points that are not on \\(D\\).  \nThe only lines left that can cover them are the \\(n-4\\) non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all of which are either horizontal or, if they were of slope \\(-1\\), would coincide with \\(D\\)).  \nEach such line meets column 3 in at most one point, so at most \\(n-4\\) points of \\(C_{3}\\setminus\\{(3,n-2)\\}\\) can be covered.  \nSince \\(|C_{3}\\setminus\\{(3,n-2)\\}| = n-3 > n-4\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\), a contradiction arises.\n\nTherefore for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with **two or more sunny lines cannot exist**, and we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 1\\qquad (n\\ge4)}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Existence of the admissible values  \n\n*4.1  \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*4.2  \\(k=1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y = x-(n-1) \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) passes through the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\), both belonging to \\(T_{n}\\).  \nEvery other point of \\(T_{n}\\) has abscissa at most \\(n-1\\) and is therefore covered by the appropriate vertical line.  \nThus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n*4.3  \\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\).*  \nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y = x,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y = -2x+5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nA direct substitution shows that each of the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of them:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n(a,b) & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1}\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{2}\\\\\n(1,3) & L_{3}\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{3}\\\\\n(2,2) & L_{1}\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nHence all points are covered and all three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of any other values  \n\n*5.1  The case \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\).*  \nWith only one non‑sunny line we could at most cover two of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) (the only points of \\(T_{3}\\) not lying on the forced diagonal).  \nThe third point would remain uncovered, so \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n*5.2  The case \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \nFrom (3.1) we already have \\(k\\le1\\).  \nThe constructions of Sections 4.1 and 4.2 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  \nTherefore no other integer \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final classification  \n\nCombining the existence results with the impossibility proofs we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3,\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4,\\quad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThis list contains **all** non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  "
