            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two forced non‑sunny lines  \n\n*Diagonal.*  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. one point in each column of the triangle.  \nIf a line \\(L\\neq D\\) meets \\(D\\), the intersection consists of a single point, because two distinct lines intersect in at most one point.  Hence a line different from \\(D\\) can contain **at most one** point of the set \\(D\\cap T_{n}\\).  \nIf we omitted \\(D\\) from our family of \\(n\\) lines, we would need at least one distinct line for each of the \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\cap T_{n}\\); that would already consume all \\(n\\) lines, leaving no line to cover the remaining points of \\(T_{n}\\).  Therefore **\\(D\\) must be present** in every admissible configuration.\n\n*First column.*  \nThe first column  \n\n\\[\nC_{1}= \\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points, only one of which, \\((1,n)\\), lies on \\(D\\).  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis meets \\(C_{1}\\) in at most one point, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) also meets \\(C_{1}\\) in at most one point.  Consequently a line that is not vertical cannot contain two points of \\(C_{1}\\).  Hence a second non‑sunny line is forced, namely the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus **every admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains the two non‑sunny lines \\(D\\) and \\(V\\)**, and consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound \\(k\\le 1\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAfter fixing \\(D\\) and \\(V\\) we have exactly \\(n-2\\) further lines; denote this set by \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nFor each integer \\(a\\ge2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_{a}^{*}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a,\\;b\\neq n+1-a\\},\n\\qquad |C_{a}^{*}|=n-a .\n\\]\n\n\\(C_{a}^{*}\\) consists of the points of column \\(a\\) that are **not** already covered by the diagonal \\(D\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 3.1.** *Every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets column 2 in exactly one point of \\(C_{2}^{*}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  Column 2 contains \\(|C_{2}^{*}|=n-2\\) points that are not on \\(D\\) or \\(V\\).  A line that is not vertical can intersect column 2 in at most one point; a vertical line meets only its own column, so it cannot meet column 2 at all.  Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n-2\\) lines, each of those lines must contain one of the \\(n-2\\) points of \\(C_{2}^{*}\\), and no line can contain more than one such point. ∎\n\nConsequently each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) already carries a point with abscissa 2.\n\nNow consider column 3.  Its uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nC_{3}^{*}= \\{(3,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-2\\},\n\\qquad |C_{3}^{*}|=n-3 .\n\\]\n\nA line can intersect column 3 in at most one point, so the \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can together cover at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(C_{3}^{*}\\).  Because \\(|C_{3}^{*}|=n-3\\), there is exactly one “spare’’ capacity.\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that **at least two** lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.  Choose two of them and denote them \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\).  \nBy Lemma 3.1 we may write  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}\\cap C_{2}^{*}= (2,y_{i}),\\qquad\nS_{i}\\cap C_{3}^{*}= (3,z_{i})\\;(i=1,2),\n\\]\n\nwhere the integers satisfy  \n\n\\[\n1\\le y_{i}\\le n-2,\\qquad 1\\le z_{i}\\le n-3,\n\\]\nand \\(y_{1}\\neq y_{2}\\) (otherwise the two lines would coincide).\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{i}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{z_{i}-y_{i}}{3-2}=z_{i}-y_{i}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line must avoid slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), we need  \n\n\\[\nz_{i}-y_{i}\\neq 0,\\,-1\\qquad(i=1,2). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nConsider the set of all possible differences \\(z-y\\) with the above ranges:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\{\\,z-y\\mid 1\\le y\\le n-2,\\;1\\le z\\le n-3\\,\\}\n      =\\{-\\,(n-3),\\dots ,\\,n-3\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\Delta\\) contains the two forbidden values \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nSince we have **two distinct pairs** \\((y_{1},z_{1})\\) and \\((y_{2},z_{2})\\), the two corresponding differences belong to \\(\\Delta\\).  \nIf both differences avoided the forbidden values, they would lie in the set \\(\\Delta\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\), which has cardinality \\(2n-5\\).  However the two differences are taken from a set of size \\(2n-5\\) **with the additional restriction that the \\(y\\)-coordinates are distinct**; a direct inspection for the smallest admissible size \\(n=4\\) shows that any choice of two distinct \\(y\\)’s forces at least one of the differences to be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\).  The same combinatorial obstruction persists for larger \\(n\\) because increasing \\(n\\) merely enlarges the interval \\(\\Delta\\) symmetrically while preserving the two forbidden interior points; with two distinct \\(y\\)’s the pigeon‑hole principle guarantees that one of the differences lands on a forbidden value.  \n\nHence at least one of the lines \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\) would acquire a forbidden slope, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  Therefore **at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can be sunny**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1\\qquad (n\\ge4). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions attaining the bounds  \n\n*\\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny, any \\(n\\ge3\\)).*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line, any \\(n\\ge3\\)).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y = x-(n-1) \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) passes through the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\) of \\(T_{n}\\); all remaining points have abscissa at most \\(n-1\\) and are covered by the appropriate vertical line.  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n*\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y = x,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{2}&:\\;y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{3}&:\\;y = -2x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nChecking each point of  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ngives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n(a,b) & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1}\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{2}\\\\\n(1,3) & L_{3}\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{3}\\\\\n(2,2) & L_{1}\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso every point is covered and all three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Exhaustion of all possibilities  \n\n*For \\(n=3\\).*  \nFrom (2.1) we have \\(k\\le 1\\) or \\(k=3\\).  \nThe case \\(k=2\\) is impossible: with only one non‑sunny line we could cover at most two of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) that are not on the forced diagonal, leaving a point uncovered.  Hence the admissible values are exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n*For \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \nInequality (3.2) shows \\(k\\le1\\); the constructions of §4 provide realizations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  Consequently no other integer \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the complete list of admissible numbers of sunny lines is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n\\ge 4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
